<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">McCarthyism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1940s–1950s</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Second Red Scare</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">McCarthyism was the practice of accusing and investigating left-wing Americans for communist and Soviet influence on American institutions and for Soviet espionage, from the late 1940s through the 1950s. It began before Senator Joseph McCarthy's fame, with President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-s-truman">Harry S. Truman</a>'s 1947 order screening federal employees for loyalty, and its best-known forms were McCarthy's own investigations and the hearings of the House Un-American Activities Committee. Many lives were ruined by blacklists, dismissals and verdicts later overturned. In 1954 the Senate voted 67 to 22 to condemn McCarthy, and rulings of the Supreme Court under Earl Warren helped bring the Second Red Scare to an end.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mccarthyism.jpg" alt="McCarthyism" width="330" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: United States Senate · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Welch-McCarthy-Hearings.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
